## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of multi-environment deployments without code duplication using provider aliases and module iteration.

## Key Concepts
- Provider aliases
- Module instantiation with for_each
- Per-environment variable files and backends
- Terraform plan/apply targeting to avoid cross-env changes

## Code Example
```terraform
provider "aws" {
  alias  = "prod"
  region = var.prod_region
}
provider "aws" {
  alias  = "dev"
  region = var.dev_region
}
variable "prod_region" { default = "us-east-1" }
variable "dev_region"  { default = "us-west-2" }
variable "environments" {
  type = map(string)
  default = { prod = "prod", dev = "dev" }
}
module "network" {
  for_each = var.environments
  source   = "./modules/network"
  providers = { aws = aws[each.value] }
  # inputs per env
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you add a new environment without touching existing state?
- What are the trade-offs of using separate backends versus workspaces for this pattern?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of provider aliases and per-environment module instantiation in Terraform.

## Key Concepts
- Provider aliases and module-level provider mappings
- For_each environment deployment and target verification
- Safe plan/apply separation per environment

## Code Example
```javascript
provider "aws" {
  alias = \"dev\"
  region = \"us-west-2\"
}
provider "aws" {
  alias = \"prod\"
  region = \"us-east-1\"
}
variable \"environments\" { type = \"map(string)\"; default = { prod = \"prod\"; staging = \"staging\" } }

module \"vpc\" {
  for_each = var.environments
  source   = \"./modules/vpc\"
  providers = {
    aws = each.key == \"prod\" ? aws.prod : aws.dev
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test rollback if prod plan uses the wrong alias?
- How can you enforce alias usage at plan time to prevent mix-ups?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement policy-based gating and cross-account state references in production workflows.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform Cloud and Sentinel policy gating
- remote_state across AWS accounts
- per-environment workspaces (qa/prod)
- CI integration for plan upload and gate checks

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal Sentinel-like policy (illustrative)
import "tfplan/v2"
import "strings"

let prodChange = false
for (rc in tfplan.resource_changes) {
  if (strings.contains(rc.address, ".prod.")) {
    if (rc.change.actions.includes("create") || rc.change.actions.includes("update") || rc.change.actions.includes("delete")) {
      prodChange = true
    }
  }
}
function main() { return !prodChange }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to handle multi-resource prod groups with blue/green deployments?
- What failure modes exist if remote_state is unavailable during plan?
